('wikiloc', '184678870', 'https://www.wikiloc.com/hiking-trails/coll-de-paracolls-and-pilo-de-bellmaig-from-route-de-la-batllie-a-vertical-kilometre-184678870', 'Coll de Paracolls and Piló de Bellmaig from Route de la Batllie - A ''vertical kilometre''', '{"en":"Vertical Kilometre from Route de la Batllie","ca":"Quilòmetre vertical des de Route de la Batllie","es":"Kilómetro vertical desde Route de la Batllie"}'::jsonb, 'Coll de Paracolls and Piló de Bellmaig from Route de la Batllie - A ''vertical kilometre'' Hiking trail in Barri d''Avall, Occitanie (France). Download its GPS track and follow the itinerary on a map.  
 
The Piló de Bellmaig is a well known peak to many who live in or visit the Vallespir. It''s particularly well know due to the annual ''KV Belmaig'' challenge. This is a race from Arles-sur Tech to the summit via the most direct route, which happens to be exactly one vertical kilometre (kilomètre vertical) in ascent, with the fastest competitors reaching the top in under 40 minutes! 
 
The route outlined below follows a different route the summit, but generally follows back down the route of the challenge. Whichever route you choose, you still have to climb a ''vertical kilometre'', you just might need to cover a bit more horizontal distance instead! 
 
I usually start from the car park at the Centre Sud Canigó Sports et Pleine Nature , although it was closed due to landscape maintenance on the day I recorded this trail. Instead, there is parking Route de la Batllie and I chose to start a bit further up. 
 
You leave the tarmac where the GR10 strikes-off east into the woods, quickly swinging south east as it climbs. The first stage of the climb is a continuous hard pull up to the Coll de Paracolls at 902 m, however the rewards increase with every meter you climb as the views of down the valley start to reveal themselves. 
 
Shortly before reaching the coll, you will pass the start of the path to leading east towards the Batterie de Santa Engràcia. This is a route for another day, but definitely worth doing if you have not already. 
 
At the signpost on the Coll de Paracolls, we leave the GR10 behind and scramble up the short rock wall (2 m max.) and onto the path which takes us the to the summit of Belmaig - still another 379 m above us. The path is narrower than the GR10, but well marked with ''yellow'' indicators / signage. From this point the views really start to open up as the trees thin-out, allowing you to look back towards Amelie-les-Bains, Ceret, the plain of Roussillon and the Mediterranean beyond. You will have spectacular views of the Canigou massif to the north and down into Spain to the south. 
 
The sign says that this stretch will take you around one hour and 10 minutes, but I always seem to manage it in around 40 minutes. Maybe it''s because the views are so good that you don''t notice the climb and you keep pushing forward to see more. However long it takes you, just as you start to notice the steepness of the final section of path, the Catalan flag comes into view and you have reached you destination. If the views were spectacular before, they are even more so on the top. 
 
I don''t have the creative ability to fully describe the panorama that confronts you (please see the photos instead), however I can say that, if the weather is good, you can plan on spending quite a bit of time up here listing all of the things you can see, from the Costa Brava to the Plain of Rousillon, from Montbolo past the Canigou to the Pic de Costabona - it''s a long list! 
 
Once you manage to drag yourself away from the summit, you have a few options for your return. I like to come descend via the heavily wooded slopes to the north west. It''s a long, relentless descent, generally following the current ''ascent'' of the KV Belmaig, but it''s a lovely path and feels quite different to your earlier ascent. 
 
The main punctuation point on the way down is Can Rigall. It''s a privately owned house and there''s nothing to see as you pass- by, however as you descend towards it, you do catch aerial glimpses the house and grounds and it does make you wonder what it''s like to live so high up! 
 
After Can Rigall, just keep following the signs for Arles by the direct route. Eventually, and with aching legs by this point, the path deposits you on and broad forestry road, which if you follow this north east, will bring you back to where your started at the start of your own ''vertical kilometre'' challenge! 
 
Final thought: 1000 m of ascent, followed by 1000 m of descent. Does that count as 2 vertical kilometres???? 
 
 
As usual, if you like this route, why not give it a clap, make a comment or leave a review? 
